                <h1 class="content-title">Client-Side Magic: <span class="gradient-text">UI Policies &amp; Client Scripts</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Learn how to dynamically validate, show, hide, and control form fields directly in the user's browser 
                    using no-code UI Policies and JavaScript client APIs (`g_form` &amp; `g_user`).
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: Client Concept -->
            <section class="content-section" id="client-concept">
                <h2>The Client-Side Philosophy (The Restaurant Dining Room)</h2>
                <p>
                    In web architecture, ServiceNow is divided into two distinct execution environments:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Client-Side (The Dining Room)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Runs entirely inside the <strong>user's web browser</strong> (Chrome, Safari, Edge). 
                            Executes instantly with zero server lag. Responsible for visual changes, field validation, 
                            and guiding the user through data entry before submission.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Server-Side (The Kitchen)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Runs on the <strong>ServiceNow cloud server &amp; database</strong>. 
                            Responsible for heavy calculations, database commits, triggering integrations, 
                            and security governance (covered on Page 6).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: UI Policies -->
            <section class="content-section" id="ui-policies">
                <h2>UI Policies (The No-Code Superpower)</h2>
                <p>
                    A <strong>UI Policy</strong> is a declarative, zero-code rule that dynamically changes how fields behave 
                    on a form based on conditions.
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The 3 Core Field Attributes</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            A UI Policy Action can set any field to:
                            <br>• <strong>Mandatory (True/False):</strong> Forces user to fill the field before saving.
                            <br>• <strong>Visible (True/False):</strong> Shows or hides the field dynamically.
                            <br>• <strong>Read-Only (True/False):</strong> Locks the field from being edited.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The "Reverse if False" Checkbox</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            When checked, if the condition stops being true, ServiceNow <em>automatically</em> reverses the action 
                            (e.g., if State changes from *Resolved* back to *In Progress*, the Close Notes field automatically hides again).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Golden Rule: Prefer UI Policies over Scripts</div>
                    <p>
                        Always use a UI Policy instead of writing JavaScript code whenever you only need to toggle Mandatory, 
                        Visible, or Read-Only properties. UI Policies are faster, require no maintenance, and load seamlessly on mobile apps.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: Client Scripts -->
            <section class="content-section" id="client-scripts">
                <h2>Client Scripts (JavaScript in the Browser)</h2>
                <p>
                    When form logic requires math calculations, string validation (e.g. checking phone number regex), 
                    or custom alert dialogs, developers write <strong>Client Scripts</strong> in standard JavaScript.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Every Client Script belongs to one of <strong>4 Execution Types</strong>:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Script Type</th>
                            <th>When It Runs</th>
                            <th>Typical Real-World Use Case</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>onLoad()</code></td>
                            <td>Runs immediately when the form finishes rendering on screen.</td>
                            <td>Displaying an informational banner if the ticket belongs to a VIP customer.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>onChange()</code></td>
                            <td>Runs whenever the user modifies a specific watched field value.</td>
                            <td>If the user changes <em>Urgency</em> to "High", automatically recalculate priority and populate an SLA warning.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>onSubmit()</code></td>
                            <td>Runs when the user clicks *Submit*, *Save*, or *Update* before data is sent to the server.</td>
                            <td>Validating that a custom end-date is after the start-date; returning <code>false</code> halts submission.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>onCellEdit()</code></td>
                            <td>Runs when an agent edits a field directly inside a List view grid.</td>
                            <td>Preventing users from changing a State to "Closed" directly from the List view without opening the form.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: g_form API -->
            <section class="content-section" id="g-form-api">
                <h2>The <code>g_form</code> (GlideForm) API Reference</h2>
                <p>
                    The <code>g_form</code> JavaScript object is pre-instantiated by ServiceNow on every form page. 
                    It gives you complete programmatic control over form elements:
                </p>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-get-set">Values &amp; State</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-messages">Messages &amp; Alerts</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-example-script">Complete Script Example</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-get-set">
                        <h3>Getting &amp; Setting Field Values</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Client Script)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Get the value entered in a field
var category = g_form.getValue('category');

// Set a field value programmatically
g_form.setValue('short_description', 'Auto-generated: Network switch outage');

// Toggle mandatory, visible, and read-only states
g_form.setMandatory('work_notes', true);
g_form.setVisible('u_hardware_serial', false);
g_form.setReadOnly('priority', true);

// Clear out a field value
g_form.clearValue('assigned_to');</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-messages">
                        <h3>Displaying User Messages &amp; Field Errors</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Client Script)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Top-of-form blue informational banner
g_form.addInfoMessage('This user is a VIP. Expedited SLA applies.');

// Top-of-form red error banner
g_form.addErrorMessage('Please fill out all mandatory fields before resolving.');

// Inline red error attached directly beneath a specific input box
g_form.showFieldMsg('u_phone_number', 'Please enter a valid 10-digit number', 'error');

// Remove messages
g_form.clearMessages();</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-example-script">
                        <h3>Example: `onSubmit` Date Validation Script</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">onSubmit Client Script</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>function onSubmit() {
    var startDate = g_form.getValue('start_date');
    var endDate = g_form.getValue('end_date');

    if (startDate && endDate && endDate < startDate) {
        g_form.addErrorMessage('End Date cannot be before Planned Start Date!');
        g_form.showFieldMsg('end_date', 'Invalid date sequence', 'error');
        return false; // Aborts form submission and keeps user on screen
    }

    return true; // Allows submission to proceed to cloud server
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: g_user API -->
            <section class="content-section" id="g-user-api">
                <h2>The <code>g_user</code> (GlideUser) API Reference</h2>
                <p>
                    The <code>g_user</code> object contains read-only session data about the currently logged-in user:
                </p>

                <div class="code-block">
                    <div class="code-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (g_user Methods)</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Access user identity properties
var currentUserId = g_user.userID;     // 32-character sys_id of the logged-in user
var currentUsername = g_user.userName; // User login username (e.g. 'admin')
var firstName = g_user.firstName;

// Check user security roles
if (g_user.hasRole('admin')) {
    g_form.addInfoMessage('Welcome Administrator! Developer mode active.');
}

if (!g_user.hasRole('itil')) {
    // Hide internal-only field for non-fulfiller employees
    g_form.setVisible('u_internal_budget_code', false);
}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: Best Practices -->
            <section class="content-section" id="best-practices">
                <h2>Client-Side Best Practices &amp; Critical Anti-Patterns</h2>
                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>❌ Never Run Synchronous GlideRecord on Client</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Calling <code>var gr = new GlideRecord(...)</code> in a Client Script makes synchronous database round-trips 
                            that completely freeze the user's browser screen until the query returns. Always use <strong>GlideAjax</strong> instead!
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>✅ Check `isLoading` in `onChange` Scripts</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Always include <code>if (isLoading || newValue === '') return;</code> at the top of <code>onChange</code> scripts 
                            to avoid running costly scripts when the page is merely initial-loading.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

